      "Theme: The Power of Starting Small, Staying Human, and Holding the Vision",
      "Dear Younger Tracey,",
      "To the young woman staring at her laptop at midnight, wondering if anyone will ever take her seriously. This letter is for you.",
      "You’re standing at the edge of something bold, something beautiful. It doesn’t feel like it yet, but you are exactly where you need to be.",
      "You’ve just walked away from a job that paid regularly to chase a dream no one quite understands yet: teaching science to children through experiments, storytelling, and laughter.",
      "In Africa? People will say it’s not scalable. Some will smile politely and never call back. But this spark, this strange, stubborn idea will one day ignite a movement. Before the wins, let’s talk about the mess.",
      "There will be days you run out of money. Not just business funds, your money. You’ll sit in your car after a pitch meeting that didn’t go well, questioning your intelligence, your worth, and your calling. You’ll plan events that barely anyone shows up for. You’ll pack kits in your living room and carry equipment in your car boot, wondering if this is really going anywhere.",
      "And still, you’ll keep going. Because eventually, one of those five children at that first failed event will write to you and say it changed how she sees herself. One parent will send you a voice note in tears, thanking you for making science real for their daughter. One school will say yes. Then another.",
      "You’ll start to see what you’re really building: not just a business, but a mirror for African children to see what’s possible. Your first big lesson? Don’t romanticize struggle, but don’t fear it either. Ask for help. Take the small wins seriously. The people who believe in you will show up.",
      "Here’s what I wish you had known sooner: Start scrappy, but start smart. MVP isn’t a buzzword, it’s survival. You don’t need perfect branding to test a great idea. Rejection isn’t personal, it’s redirection. You’ll apply to 50 grants before one says yes. Don’t let the “no”s steal your fire. You are your own credibility. Walk into those rooms, even when they mistake you for the assistant, and take your seat at the table. You belong.",
      "You don’t have to do it all. Delegate. Hire slowly, but with heart. Your team will be your second greatest asset, after your vision. Your customers are your compass. Every parent who buys a kit, every child who builds a model volcano, will teach you how to grow. Listen. And one more thing: don’t underestimate how much you matter in this journey.",
      "Funders, partners, and collaborators won’t just buy into the kits or the platform, they’ll buy into you. So don’t just tell the polished story. Tell the messy one. The late-night tears, the risks, the pivots. That’s where the real credibility lives. Your faith, your children, your love for your continent, these aren’t distractions. They are your fuel. They’ll give you the courage to show up again and again, even when the numbers don’t make sense, and the path feels uncertain.",
      "So when it gets hard (and it will) remember: you’re planting seeds. It may take time, but the forest is coming.",
      "With love and deep belief,",
