    "excerpt": "The 15th May 2015. That’s the date my heart took a deep breath and stepped into a path I didn’t fully understand, but one I deeply believed in. I founded Nomad Footprints Films with nothing but a borrowed camera, a head full of stories, and a fire in my belly. I didn’t have a blu",
    "content": [
      "To my fellow Founders",
      "The 15th May 2015. That’s the date my heart took a deep breath and stepped into a path I didn’t fully understand, but one I deeply believed in. I founded Nomad Footprints Films with nothing but a borrowed camera, a head full of stories, and a fire in my belly. I didn’t have a blueprint, a business plan, or a network—I had a vision. A vision of African stories told by Africans, in our own voices, with dignity, honesty, and depth.",
      "I didn’t know where the path would lead. What I did know was those stories — our stories — were being lost, distorted, or left untold. I was a young Maasai woman, full of questions, dreams, and a burning desire to document the truth of our lived experiences, from the pastoralist plains of Laikipia to the climate-affected banks of the Ewaso Ng’iro River. That calling—to use film as a mirror, an archive, and a bridge—has shaped my life in ways I could never have imagined.",
      "Today, I reflect on this journey not just to celebrate the wins, but to share the moments that almost made me give up, and what carried me through. In the early days, I faced skepticism not just from the outside world but from within my own community. As a Maasai woman, the idea of being behind the camera—let alone owning a film company—was unheard of. Many expected me to fail, politely or otherwise. And some days, I believed them.",
      "It wasn’t always beautiful. There were long stretches without funding. Promises from partners that went unfulfilled. Burnout. Imposter syndrome. I learned the hard way that passion alone can’t pay your team or feed your family. There were days I questioned if I had made the right choice, and nights when I cried quietly, thinking of all I had sacrificed.",
      "Looking back, I see that it wasn’t just about starting a film company. It was about reclaiming our narrative spaces and ensuring future generations of Maa-speaking communities would recognize themselves in the histories we leave behind. Nomad Footprints Films became my way of refusing invisibility.",
      "Between 2019 and 2024, while serving as Project Manager and Coordinator at Maasai Cultural Heritage, I came face to face with the depth and urgency of our work. Coordinating day-to-day operations, managing teams, writing grants, approving budgets—it was a crash course in leadership under pressure. But my favorite days were always the ones spent with elders, women, and youth, capturing oral stories on dusty roads and sacred lands, laughing behind the camera, and editing late into the night. We weren’t just making films. We were safeguarding memory.",
      "I learned that leadership in community storytelling means holding space for many truths. It means listening more than you speak. It means navigating the emotional weight of trauma, resistance, and hope—all at once. And it means failing sometimes. I’ve missed funding deadlines, dealt with broken equipment in the field, cried over hard drives that refused to power on, and questioned whether I was really cut out for this.",
      "But I’ve also witnessed the quiet power of impact: young girls in various marginalized communities telling me they want to be a filmmaker after watching Our Land, Our Life, River or Brown Waters, MY FGM STORY, and ENCHUKUNOTO; a health worker in Busia seeing herself represented with dignity in Data-Driven Healthcare; an elder in Laikipia, moved to tears after watching ENCHUKUNOTO (The Return), thanking us for capturing the meaning of homecoming. These moments fuel me more than any award ever could.",
      "Over the years, I’ve been blessed to direct and produce documentaries across themes of climate justice, Indigenous land rights, maternal healthcare, and community peace. From The River of Brown Waters to Ntumo Nabulaa (Echoes of Peace), my lens has been guided by one question: Whose story is not being told, and why?",
      "Founding and growing Nomad Footprints Films has taught me many lessons, but one stands above the rest: collaboration is our greatest currency. Every film we’ve made has been rooted in collective effort—from the grassroots communities who opened their lives to our cameras, to our editing team working from tiny corners of Kenya and beyond, to funders and partners who believed in our vision when we had little more than passion.",
      "To African founders walking their own journey",
      "Start with what you have. Your voice, your story, your lived experience—these are not limitations; they are your wealth. And when the road feels too long or lonely, remember that leadership doesn’t mean knowing everything. It means choosing to grow in public, to learn with humility, and to keep showing up even when it’s hard.",
      "Surround yourself with people who believe in your vision but are not afraid to challenge you. Learn to speak the language of funders, but never lose the voice of your community. And most importantly—document everything. Not just for reports or proposals, but for the archive of our becoming.",
      "The journey of Nomad Footprints is still unfolding. We’re now exploring character-driven stories inside hospitals, documenting women-led regeneration in climate-vulnerable areas, and building stronger partnerships across the continent. But at the heart of it all, we are still doing what we set out to do in 2015: telling African stories, by us and for us.",
      "To my younger self: you were scared, and that’s okay. You did it anyway—and that’s what matters. To the next generation of storytellers and founders: the world needs your lens. Keep walking. Keep documenting. Your footprints matter.",
      "In solidarity,",
      "Laissa Malih,",
      "Founder, Nomad Footprints Films"
    ],
    "bio": "My names are Laissa Malih, a visionary Laikipian Maasai filmmaker and the first female filmmaker in my community, with over eight years of experience in documentary filmmaking, strategic project management, and donor engagement. Adept at linking, amplifying, and scaling marginalized voices across diverse cultures and communities in Kenya and globally through compelling storytelling and impactful visual narratives. I am a proven leader with expertise in fundraising, grant and proposal writing, donor relations, program development, budget management, and cross-sector collaboration. I am skilled in overseeing complex projects, managing teams, and driving initiatives that foster social impact, cultural preservation, and policy advocacy. I am passionate about leveraging media and strategic partnerships to influence positive change at local, regional, and global levels."
