    "excerpt": "I’m Kevin Irungu, co-founder of ChipuRobo, and if you’re the young builder I was—burning the midnight oil watching tutorial after tutorial on how to bring hardware and code together, yet nothing is working—then this letter is for you.",
    "content": [
      "To the 21-Year-Old Dreaming in Zeros and Ones,",
      "I’m Kevin Irungu, co-founder of ChipuRobo, and if you’re the young builder I was—burning the midnight oil watching tutorial after tutorial on how to bring hardware and code together, yet nothing is working—then this letter is for you.",
      "You’re 21, and you’ve found yourself in a noisy cyber café or hunched over a borrowed laptop, hitting compile for the 20th time, wondering why that Arduino still won’t respond. You’ve probably tried rigging sensors with wires that barely fit, soldered things that shouldn’t be soldered, and followed YouTube tutorials that assume you have tools you can’t even afford.",
      "You’re frustrated. Not just because the code won’t run, but because you don’t see anyone around you doing what you’re trying to do. The world tells you “innovation” lives in Silicon Valley, but your heart is set on proving it can grow in Nairobi—with scraps, with grit, with vision.",
      "Let me tell you: it’s okay that nothing is working right now. Every error is a clue. Every “failure” is fertilizer. You’re not behind. You’re just early. When we started ChipuRobo, we didn’t have much. Just an idea and a passion to bring robotics and AI to young Africans who rarely see themselves as builders of technology. We didn’t know how to structure a business, let alone an educational movement. But we showed up—over and over. We taught in schools with no internet. We 3D-printed parts from recycled bottles. We learned from every mistake. And we built something real.",
      "The truth is, your biggest resource isn’t funding or perfect equipment. It’s your obsession to make things work. It’s your audacity to try anyway. That stubborn belief that this—whatever this is—could be better.",
      "Here’s what I wish I knew earlier:",
      "You don’t need to start with everything—start with something.",
      "Community isn’t a luxury; it’s a necessity. Find people who believe in crazy ideas like yours.",
      "Impact is built in layers. Teach. Build. Fail. Teach again. Keep moving.",
      "Your background doesn’t disqualify you. It uniquely qualifies you.",
      "If you’re wondering whether this journey is worth it, it is. One day, you’ll look around and see a robot built by kids who had never held a screwdriver before. You’ll witness a deaf student write her first line of Python code and smile with pride. You’ll see blind learners navigate with sensors you helped configure. And you’ll remember every crash, every delay, every night you almost quit.",
      "That’s when you’ll know: it was all worth it. So keep going. Keep building. And remember—you’re not just coding a project. You’re wiring a future.",
      "With brotherhood and belief,",
      "Kevin Irungu",
      "Co-founder, ChipuRobo"
    ]
